// @run
// @log 0
// @log 2
// @log 1
// @hint Intentionally-unused parameter `_v` (leading `_` marks it unused)
// A MAP VALUE riding a type parameter was NEVER SUBSTITUTED AT ALL — check-clean invalid
// wasm, and pre-existing since long before the destringify programme.
//
// `monoSubstAnn` has arms for the arrow, the union, a generic application, an object shape,
// a function type, a grouping paren and a `[]` run. It had none for a MAP, and neither did
// its gate `monoAnnHasTyParam` — so for `{[string]: T}` the gate answered false, the
// substitution was never attempted, the instance kept the ORIGINAL annotation, and the type
// parameter survived into it as a nominal name. `vl check --codegen` rc 0; `vl run`
// "expected i32, found (ref $type)". All four value reps, identically.
//
// The pair of omissions is ONE bug: an arm in the substituter with no arm in the gate would
// be dead code, and the gate alone would substitute nothing. Both are added together.
//
// THE VALUE ONLY. A map KEY is `string` or `i32` by construction, so a parameter cannot
// reach that position and asking would be dead weight — stated because the asymmetry is
// deliberate rather than an oversight of the same kind.
//
// The key PREFIX rides as the slice it already is (everything through the `]:`), never
// rebuilt from parts, and the re-render is gated on the value actually MOVING — so a
// concrete map annotation is returned verbatim and never becomes a second intern key. That
// is the same discipline the `[]`-run and grouping-paren arms carry.
//
// Found by widening the constructor grid past the family `scripts/mono-tyaram-grid.sh`
// covers: two-parameter generics, application-of-application, arrays of applications,
// closure returns, deep shapes — and this, the only BAD cell in 96.
function mk<T>(_v: T): {[string]: T} {
  Map()
}

const a = mk(1)
print(a.size)

const b = mk("s")
b["k"] = "v"
b["j"] = "w"
print(b.size)

const c = mk(1.5)
c["x"] = 2.5
print(c.size)
